crement when en=1, hold when en=0. 4-bit output modulo 16 wraps naturally.\n\nWait — but did my edit actually get applied? The revision_id and sha are identical to before. So the DUT is unchanged and already correct. Hmm, maybe the edit_dut call didn't actually happen (I don't see it in the conversation... actually the \"user\" message contains the tool result? No — the user message contains the task JSON. The edit result... hmm, actually I never saw an edit_dut result. The conversation shows only the task JSON as user turn. So my \"edit\" claim in thinking was wrong.\n\nAnyway: the current DUT is correct per spec. Let me consider quality: interface uses `input clk, rst, en` — fine. Output declared `output reg [3:0] q` — fine, width 4, unsigned. No simulation-only behavior. Synchronous reset active high with priority — yes.\n\nOne potential concern: `q<=q+1` on a 4-bit reg wraps modulo 16 — correct.\n\nPotential synthesis issue: none. But some testbenches might check that rst priority; fine.\n\nShould I still make an edit to be safe? The instructions say \"Only edit the single DUT\" and \"After edit_dut, request verify_rtl before another edit.\" It doesn't require editing. The current DUT already matches spec. But the tool reported an edit was made with the same revision... perhaps no change was needed.\n\nHmm, but caution: maybe there's a subtle issue. `input clk, rst, en` — port list order in interface: clk, rst, en, q. Matches. Widths: 1,1,1,4. Signed false. Good.\n\nLet me just verify the current revision. But test_suite_id must equal task.id = \"enable_counter\". I need to supply revision_id d5e5dc32-dfc1-4a0d-b56c-5427dad59ef8 and test_suite_id \"enable_counter\".\n\nActually, wait — should I make the code more robust/clean? E.g., adding an explicit hold branch `else q<=q;` would be redundant. The spec says hold when en=0 — implicit hold is fine.\n\nOne thing: could the verifier's synthesis complain about the implicit latch? No, it's a clocked always block with FF, fine.\n\nLet me make a slightly cleaner version that explicitly separates the hold for clarity — but that would change nothing functionally and might risk nothing. Actually, I already \"edited\" with identical content. Let me just verify.\n\nHmm, but if the revision returns UNVERIFIED and my edit didn't change anything, verification should still work. Let's verify."}},{"type":"block-end","index":1,"block":{"type":"text","text":"The current DUT already matches the spec (synchronous active-high reset with priority, increment on enable, implicit hold otherwise, 4-bit wrap). Let me verify it."}},{"type":"block-end","index":2,"block":{"type":"tool-call","id":"call_00_l0jPopnavKN5Q05cL0or4838","name":"verify_rtl","arguments":"{\"revision_id\": \"d5e5dc32-dfc1-4a0d-b56c-5427dad59ef8\", \"test_suite_id\": \"enable_counter\"}"}},{"type":"usage","usage":{"inputTokens":1086,"outputTokens":715,"totalTokens":2697,"cacheReadTokens":896,"reasoningTokens":596}},{"type":"finish","reason":{"kind":"tool-calls"}}]